WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.328
The compiler is powerful, but it is not
the only safety tool.

2
00:00:03.408 --> 00:00:06.406
Teams should test boundary cases, invalid
input, empty input,

3
00:00:06.486 --> 00:00:10.559
oversized input, unusual encodings, state
transitions, and concurrency behavior.

4
00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:13.523
Unit tests and integration tests verify
intended behavior,

5
00:00:13.603 --> 00:00:17.223
while negative tests help confirm that
invalid states are rejected safely.

6
00:00:17.303 --> 00:00:20.867
Fuzzing concepts are useful defensively
around parsers, deserializers,

7
00:00:20.947 --> 00:00:23.945
file formats, protocol handling, and other
input-heavy code.

8
00:00:24.025 --> 00:00:27.305
The goal is to discover unexpected
behavior before production does.

9
00:00:27.385 --> 00:00:29.308
Fuzzing does not replace design review,

10
00:00:29.388 --> 00:00:33.235
but it can reveal assumptions that
ordinary examples and happy-path tests miss.

11
00:00:33.315 --> 00:00:34.842
Tooling can support safer code.

12
00:00:34.922 --> 00:00:36.958
Linting, formatting, dependency review,

13
00:00:37.038 --> 00:00:40.545
and specialized runtime checks can improve
consistency and visibility.

14
00:00:40.625 --> 00:00:44.188
Miri and sanitizer concepts are useful to
understand at a defensive level,

15
00:00:44.268 --> 00:00:47.719
especially when unsafe code, FFI, or
low-level boundaries are present.

16
00:00:47.799 --> 00:00:49.948
Teams do not need every tool in every
project,

17
00:00:50.028 --> 00:00:52.856
but they should choose tools that match
their risk profile.

18
00:00:52.936 --> 00:00:56.387
Code review should pay special attention
to unsafe blocks, FFI, panics,

19
00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:00.483
indexing assumptions, lifetime
workarounds, shared state, public API contracts,

20
00:01:00.563 --> 00:01:01.864
and concurrency behavior.

21
00:01:01.944 --> 00:01:05.960
Release readiness should include
dependency records and review of build settings.

22
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.510
Findings from tests, reviews,

23
00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.380
and incidents should feed back into safer
patterns, not just one-time patches.

24
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.458
The strongest teams treat review findings
as design feedback.

25
00:01:14.538 --> 00:01:17.762
If a bug keeps appearing around indexing,
parsing, or shared state,

26
00:01:17.842 --> 00:01:21.122
the answer may be a better abstraction
rather than another local fix.

27
00:01:21.202 --> 00:01:22.390
Over time, tests, tools,

28
00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.260
and reviews should make the safer pattern
easier to choose than the fragile one.
